From c2c5ab70cf9ce5b97119a8391897529e3893a88d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Still <mikal@stillhq.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 21:58:46 +1000
Subject: [PATCH v2 4/4] ui/spice: also split damage on unchanged columns

The mirror diff splits damage where at least 32 rows did not change,
but changes side by side in the same rows still end up in one box.
When the guest reports more damage than what changed, that box spans
everything in between: with a video and a clock on the same rows of a
Linux virtio-gpu guest that flushes the whole plane, every clock tick
turns the 480x360 video draw into a 639x360 one, which the spice
server has to send outside the video stream.

Split each box where at least 32 whole columns did not change, and
diff each remaining part again, as it may cover fewer rows. Finding the
columns costs one pass over the box, which stops as soon as no gap is
left: for a box that changed everywhere, such as a video, after its
first row.

With the clock beside the video in a 1280x800 guest, streaming-video
=filter, the display channel carries 5.0 MB instead of 6.5 MB over
28 seconds, the clock is sent as its own small draws, and the p95
damage to command latency drops from 12ms to 1.2ms. Without
streaming, 639x360 draws are no longer sent. The cost is a column
scan of each box that has a gap; for two single pixel wide changes
640 columns apart on every row of a 1920x1080 frame, the diff takes
0.7ms instead of 0.2ms, still less than the draws it avoids.

Prompt: Consider splitting damage on horizontal gaps too, so that side
by side changes in the same rows are not merged into one box, and only
implement it if it is cheap and measurably useful.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context, low effort) <noreply@anthropic.com>
